   "h": "Questions engineering, validation and quality teams ask",
   "items": [
    {
     "q": "Our URS is a draft. Is it too early to send it?",
     "a": "No. A draft is the best time. Annex 15, 3.2 says the essential elements of quality should be built in at the URS stage, and the cheapest change is a sentence. We return the compliance matrix with gaps, conflicts and untestable requirements, each with a proposed wording. The URS stays yours to approve."
    },
    {
     "q": "Who owns DQ: you or us?",
     "a": "You do. Annex 15, 3.3 describes DQ as the documented verification that the design meets GMP and the URS, and the 2025 revision of ASTM E2500 calls for quality unit approval of it. We supply the design review report, traceability matrix and risk assessment as evidence, in your template if you have one."
    },
    {
     "q": "Our site does not use ASTM E2500. Does that change your design?",
     "a": "Not the documents; only how much is verified and repeated. Either way, Annex 15, 3.6 allows some FAT tests without repeating them at IQ/OQ, where you can show transport and installation do not affect them. The commissioning and qualification plan names those tests, and your quality unit approves the FAT protocol before execution."
    },
    {
     "q": "Which risk assessment method do you use?",
     "a": "ICH Q9(R1) does not prescribe one. For water systems we usually run a failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA) at component and function level, with your quality and engineering leads in the room. We use your template and scoring if your procedures set one. The output: critical aspects, critical design elements and the verification each needs."
    },
    {
     "q": "Can you work with our engineering firm's design?",
     "a": "Yes. We can design the water system inside a package your architect-engineer leads, issuing our P&IDs, GA drawings and utility list for their drawings. Or we review a water system design they have produced against your URS and the standards above."
    },
    {
